@import { root_paths } from "./_docs_root.mds"

A partial: the feature-knowledge load and write-back steps. D-SETTINGS-LINE: it
imports nothing from `_settings.mds`. Write-back Step 1 takes `KNOWLEDGE` from the
settings line the host's settings block resolved above, and keeps the
`KNOWLEDGE=off` skip inside its own step.

D-CMD-CONDENSE (#456): a cut here lands in every host, so each one names the line
that keeps its text in the same context. Load's closing summary restated Steps 1-3,
which each name their read, and Step 1's closing sentence restated Step 2's fallback
fields. The Knowledge spawn names what only the caller knows (root, slug, directories,
files, the two paths, the index line format and its caps); how the agent writes the
index, writes the two files directly and commits them is held by its own body
(knowledge.mds steps 6-8, Direct Write Protocol, Commit Protocol), so the spawn no
longer restates it.

The checkout-root block lives once, in `knowledge_root()`, which expands the shared
`root_paths()` step of `_docs_root.mds` (one `resolve-root.cjs` call, run once per run) and
adds the knowledge fact. `knowledge_load()` carries it,
and so does `knowledge_writeback()`, which a host with no load step uses. A host that renders
`knowledge_load()` earlier in the same command calls `knowledge_writeback_after_load()`, whose
Step 1 and Step 3 take the `{worktree}` that the load step resolved.

A host that resolves `{worktree}` earlier with `docs_root()` can call `knowledge_load_after_root()`
(implement does), which carries the load steps without a second root block. A host that calls it
then calls `knowledge_writeback_after_load()` too, and the `{worktree}` that the docs-root step
resolved serves the write-back.

The import is selective and takes `root_paths` alone: that define has no imports of its own, so
it adds one node to the scope of each define here.

@define knowledge_root():
{{root_paths()}}

All paths below are relative to `{worktree}`.
@end

@define knowledge_load_steps():
**Step 1 — Read the index cache** `{worktree}/.devflow/features/index.md`. Each line has the form `- **{slug}** — {areas} — {Use-when description}`. If `index.md` is absent or has no entry line, glob `{worktree}/.devflow/features/*/KNOWLEDGE.md` and read only the YAML frontmatter of each file.

**Step 2 — Pick relevant KBs:** select the entries whose documented area overlaps the current task (index line, or frontmatter `description` and `directories` on fallback).

**Step 3 — Read each selected KB's Rules.** `{kb}` is `{worktree}/.devflow/features/{slug}/KNOWLEDGE.md`:

1. List its `##` headings with line numbers through Bash: `command grep -n '^## ' "{kb}"`, one call for every selected `{kb}`. This only locates sections; the text of a KB comes from the Read view alone.
2. Read the `## Rules` range (its line to the next heading) with the Read tool, using `offset` and `limit`, all selected KBs in one turn, and choose the one to three bullets most relevant to the task.
3. If the KB has no `## Rules` section, choose one to three entries from its `## Anti-Patterns` or `## Gotchas` range the same way, and label them by that section's name instead of an ID.

When a KB contradicts the code you observe, **trust the code**. A missing Rules section, a missing heading list and `(none)` are legitimate states, not errors.

**Step 4 — Set FEATURE_KNOWLEDGE and FEATURE_KNOWLEDGE_RULES.** Write one block per selected KB. Paste each bullet verbatim from the Read view, never from a shell view. The path is relative to the checkout root; an agent resolves it under `WORKTREE_PATH` when one is provided.

```
--- Feature knowledge: {slug} ---
KB: .devflow/features/{slug}/KNOWLEDGE.md
Rules:
- **KB-AP-2** {bullet text, verbatim}
- **KB-INV-1** {bullet text, verbatim}
Headings: L5 Rules · L40 Overview · L62 Anti-Patterns · L118 Key Files
```

A KB with no Rules section labels its entries `Rules ({section name}):` and gives them no ID. `FEATURE_KNOWLEDGE` is these blocks; `FEATURE_KNOWLEDGE_RULES` is the same blocks without the `Headings:` line. Each spawn names the variable its recipient takes. If no KBs exist, no KBs are relevant, or `.devflow/features/` is absent, set both to `(none)`.
@end

@define knowledge_load():
### Load Feature Knowledge

{{knowledge_root()}}

{{knowledge_load_steps()}}
@end

@define knowledge_load_after_root():
### Load Feature Knowledge

{{knowledge_load_steps()}}
@end

@export knowledge_load
@export knowledge_load_after_root

@define knowledge_writeback_steps():
**Step 1 — Check the opt-out gate, with `{root}` = `{worktree}`:** take the settings line resolved above for that root, resolving it with the settings block when this run has not yet.

If the settings line says `KNOWLEDGE=off`, skip write-back entirely. The fail-closed line says `KNOWLEDGE=off` too.

**Step 2 — Write back only if at least one is true:**
- This workflow changed files in a directory that is documented by an existing feature knowledge base (a documented area changed). Knowledge bases are written through at that point, never on a background schedule.
- This workflow found durable, cross-cutting knowledge about a codebase area that future agents in that area need: patterns, anti-patterns, integration points, gotchas that a single file read does not show.

Otherwise skip write-back silently.

**Step 3 — Spawn the Knowledge agent:**

```
Agent(subagent_type="Knowledge"):
"WORKTREE_PATH: {worktree root}
FEATURE_SLUG: {slug derived from primary changed directory, kebab-case}
FEATURE_NAME: {human-readable name}
DIRECTORIES: {list of primary directories touched by this workflow}
FILES_CHANGED: {list of files changed}
<!-- curation:on -->
DECISIONS_CONTEXT: {the slice for these directories, else (none)}
<!-- curation:end -->

Write the knowledge base to:
  {worktree}/.devflow/features/{slug}/KNOWLEDGE.md

Then update the index cache:
  {worktree}/.devflow/features/index.md

Index line format: `- **{slug}** — {areas} — {Use-when description}` — at most 300 characters, the description at most 220; reword a longer one, never cut it.

Then commit the two files as your Commit Protocol directs."
```

**Step 4 — Surface an uncommitted knowledge base:**

When the Knowledge agent reports `KB_COMMIT: skipped (detached HEAD)`, tell the user in the workflow's final report, in one line, that the knowledge base was written but not committed, and name the uncommitted paths the agent listed. Never commit them yourself.

Non-blocking: if the Knowledge agent fails, log the failure and continue.
@end

@define knowledge_writeback():
### Feature Knowledge Write-Back (Conditional)

{{knowledge_root()}}

{{knowledge_writeback_steps()}}
@end

@define knowledge_writeback_after_load():
